---
import SeoLayout from '../../layouts/SeoLayout.astro';
import { personaBySlug, isPublished, personaUrl, guideUrl } from '../../lib/seo-routes';
import { APP_URL } from '../../lib/site';

const persona = personaBySlug('distributed-teams');
const title = 'Live translation for distributed teams';
const description =
  'Recurring calls across three or four languages, without a per-language tariff or a ' +
  'procurement cycle. Everyone speaks their own language and nobody waits.';
---

<SeoLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={personaUrl(persona.slug)}
  noindex={!isPublished(persona)}
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Four people, four languages, everyone speaking, nobody waiting. That is a different problem
      from a conference — and it is the one the large platforms are not solving, because their
      customer is the event.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the native tooling struggles here</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Google Meet supports one language pair per meeting</strong>, English-anchored. A
        team with Polish, Spanish and German speakers cannot use it — an architectural limit, not a
        pricing one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Teams Interpreter does not work in ad hoc meetings</strong>, covers 10 languages,
        and requires Microsoft 365 Copilot rather than Teams Premium — roughly three times the cost
        most teams budget for. External contractors are excluded entirely.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Zoom translates to captions only.</strong> Reading captions while trying to contribute
        to a fast discussion leaves people passive.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What actually helps</h2>
    <p>
      Before buying anything, the free changes usually do more: decisions in writing, channel
      translation on, meetings recorded with transcripts, agendas circulated in advance. Live
      translation earns its cost in one place — <strong
        >the meetings where discussion happens</strong
      >. Design debates, retrospectives, planning. Not status updates, which should be written.
    </p>

    <h2>What we offer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Symmetric conversation</strong>, not one speaker into many passive listeners</li>
      <li>
        <strong>No per-language tariff</strong> — a four-language call is not four times the price
      </li>
      <li><strong>Credits that never expire</strong>, which matters when usage is irregular</li>
      <li><strong>Published pricing</strong>, no sales call</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When you should not choose us</h2>
    <p>
      If your team is two languages with English on one side, everyone is internal and licensed, and
      calls run under 90 minutes, Google Meet already does this and it is included in what you pay.
      Use it. If you need twelve active participants, we cap at four and you should look elsewhere
      rather than work around it.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to start</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('run-meetings-with-a-multilingual-team')}
          >How to run meetings with a multilingual team</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('remote-team-language-barrier-solutions')}
          >Practical fixes for a remote team's language barrier</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('team-standup-different-languages')}
          >Running a standup when the team speaks different languages</a
        >
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="cta"><a href={APP_URL}>Try it on a real call</a> — no contract, no minimum.</p>
  </article>
</SeoLayout>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-width: 72ch;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 3rem 1.25rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
  li {
    margin-block: 0.5rem;
  }
  .cta {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
